1. Ringström 2009 abstract (inverted index, reconstructed): "Einstein's vacuum equations can be viewed as an initial value problem, and given initial data there is one part of spacetime, the so-called maximal globally hyperbolic development (MGHD), which is uniquely determined up to isometry. Unfortunately, it is sometimes possible to extend the spacetime beyond the MGHD in inequivalent ways. Consequently, initial data do not determine the spacetime in this sense and the theory is not deterministic. It is then natural to make the strong cosmic censorship conjecture, which states that for generic initial data, the MGHD is inextendible. Since it is unrealistic to hope to prove the conjecture in all generality, it is natural to prove the same conjecture within a class of spacetimes satisfying some symmetry condition. Here, we [consider] T^3-Gowdy spacetimes. In a previous paper, we introduced a set [of initial data] ... smooth ... proved ... is open ... C^1 0-topology. The solutions corresponding ... have the following properties: First, [they are C^2]-inextendible. Second, [for a] causal geodesic [with] time direction, it is either complete, or [a] curvature invariant, [the] Kretschmann scalar, is unbounded along [it] (in fact [the] scalar [is unbounded along] any causal curve [that] ends on [the] singularity). The purpose of the present paper [is to prove that the set is] dense [in the] C^infinity-topology. C^2..." 

